+# Answer: `fs.rmSync` options in Node 22
+
+**Question** ([QUESTION.md](QUESTION.md)): We call `fs.rmSync(dir)` to delete a folder and it throws on non-empty folders. What options does Node 22 document for `fs.rmSync`, and which one fixes this?
+
+## Short answer
+
+Use **`recursive: true`**. Add `force: true` too if the folder might not exist:
+
+```js
+fs.rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });
+```
+
+## Documented options (Node 22)
+
+Source: https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v22.x/api/fs.html#fsrmsyncpath-options (page header: Node.js v22.23.5 documentation, read 2026-11-11). Signature: `fs.rmSync(path[, options])`. Added in v14.14.0. Since v17.3.0/v16.14.0, `path` can be a `file:` URL. *(read)*
+
+| Option | Type | Default | Documented behaviour |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `force` | boolean | `false` | When `true`, exceptions are ignored if `path` does not exist. |
+| `maxRetries` | integer | `0` | Number of retries, with a linear backoff, on `EBUSY`, `EMFILE`, `ENFILE`, `ENOTEMPTY` or `EPERM`. Ignored unless `recursive` is `true`. |
+| `recursive` | boolean | `false` | If `true`, performs a recursive directory removal. In recursive mode, operations are retried on failure. |
+| `retryDelay` | integer | `100` | Milliseconds to wait between retries. Ignored unless `recursive` is `true`. |
+
+The docs describe it as "Synchronously removes files and directories (modeled on the standard POSIX `rm` utility). Returns `undefined`." *(read)*
+
+## Which option fixes it
+
+- **`recursive`** is the fix. It is the only option that makes `rmSync` remove a directory and its contents. *(read and measured)*
+- **`force`** does **not** fix it. It only suppresses the error when the path does not exist. *(read and measured)*
+- **`maxRetries`** and **`retryDelay`** only take effect when `recursive` is `true`. They help with transient errors such as `EBUSY` or `EPERM` (for example, Windows file locks). They do not fix this problem on their own. *(read)*
+
+## Measured on Node v22.23.4 (this machine)
+
+I ran a `node -e` script against a temporary directory `x/y/f`:
+
+```
+no opts: ERR_FS_EISDIR Path is a directory: rm returned EISDIR (is a directory) /tmp/rm-…/x
+exists after recursive: false
+missing no force: ENOENT
+missing with force: ok
+```
+
+Correction to the premise: without `recursive`, `rmSync` fails for **any** directory, empty or not. The error is `ERR_FS_EISDIR`, not `ENOTEMPTY`. *(measured: an empty temp dir also throws `ERR_FS_EISDIR`)*
+
+## Notes / open questions
+
+- This repo has no `rmSync` call in it (the repo holds only `QUESTION.md`), so I couldn't check the call site itself.
+- Be careful with `{ recursive: true, force: true }`: it deletes everything under `dir` and never complains. Make sure `dir` can't be empty or point at the wrong place (for example, `''` or `/`).
